<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">W. E. B. Du Bois</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as W.E.B. Du Bois, William Edward Burghardt Du Bois</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">W. E. B. Du Bois was an American sociologist, historian, writer and Pan-Africanist civil rights activist, the first African American to earn a doctorate at Harvard. In The Souls of Black Folk of 1903 he wrote that &quot;the problem of the Twentieth Century is the problem of the color line&quot;. A founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">NAACP</a>, he edited its journal The Crisis from 1910 and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, helped to organise several Pan-African Congresses. He settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> in 1961 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois.jpg" alt="W. E. B. Du Bois" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James E. Purdy / Adam Cuerden · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W.E.B._Du_Bois_by_James_E._Purdy,_1907_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>W. E. B. Du Bois</b>’s ideas of the “talented tenth” and of “double consciousness” were part of its debates, as was an open critique of Christianity.</blockquote><a href="/movement/harlem-renaissance">Harlem Renaissance · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The Harlem Renaissance of the 1920s and 1930s, whose writers, among them Langston Hughes, Claude McKay, Alain Locke and <b>W.E.B. Du Bois</b>, addressed race relations and double consciousness, was a further influence.</blockquote><a href="/movement/negritude">Négritude · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The words “the problem of the twentieth century is the problem of the colour-line”, famous from <b>W. E. B. Du Bois</b>’s The Souls of Black Folk (1903), had already appeared in the address he drafted for the London conference of 1900.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>W. E. B. Du Bois</b>, in The Souls of Black Folk (1903), read the longing for the &quot;old folks at home&quot; otherwise: as a longing for the people and traditions of Africa, where most of those enslaved in the New World had lived free before they were kidnapped…</blockquote><a href="/songs/old-folks-at-home">Old Folks at Home</a></li><li><blockquote>Frederick Douglass wrote that the songs he heard in slavery were &quot;a testimony against slavery, and a prayer to God for deliverance from chains&quot;, and <b>W. E. B. Du Bois</b>, in The Souls of Black Folk (1903), called these &quot;Sorrow Songs&quot; the &quot;singular spiritual heritage of the nation&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wade-in-the-water">Wade in the Water</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Great Barrington, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.2,-73.36&amp;year=1929" aria-label="Great Barrington on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2769 662 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of W. E. B. Du Bois"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3219 932h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3219 932h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.2,-73.36&amp;year=1929">What was here in 1929? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
